# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MailFlow — Environment Configuration
#
# 1. Copy this file:   cp .env.example .env
# 2. Fill in the values below
# 3. Generate the TLS cert (see README) or use --profile https for Let's Encrypt
# 4. Run:              docker compose up -d --build
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ── Access ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No host ports published; Traefik reaches the frontend over the `traefik`
# docker network, so APP_PORT/APP_HTTP_PORT below are unused.
APP_PORT=443
APP_HTTP_PORT=80
# off: MailFlow's nginx stops serving HTTPS on port 443 and generates no
# certificate; Traefik terminates TLS and talks HTTP to the frontend.
NGINX_TLS=off
# Traefik (1) is the only proxy in front of nginx here.
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=1
# Full URL users see in the browser — used in invite emails and CORS.
APP_URL=https://mail.corsair.tf
# ── Secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED — generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=ENC[AES256_GCM,data:TjSHB5XL4Ovt6Jwk6X6Zd1oJOYeuH5vXy59ozHoOz+4GTM5ccX7Szc1ao6JhgSyIKFyQZEzbCUck6jFipnZA7Q==,iv:gMKl/K1CkCMYGX0To4c6fQ+26SMCFu7r4cImSCoTvsc=,tag:ZPx1uEifwEC1YbYDx/0QiA==,type:str]
# REQUIRED — generate with: openssl rand -hex 16
DB_PASSWORD=ENC[AES256_GCM,data:zRa71Bnp0lZG/3XkTL/qkxfEljlt7bqrdhI6b50mGPs=,iv:rxf72Pix4rxDJT63nbSfY4bayjABwidaN0bcvHSMjGM=,tag:Mw8re5EzFKIkZDVu/mI0/A==,type:str]
# REQUIRED — generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Used to encrypt stored email credentials (passwords, OAuth tokens) at rest.
# WARNING: If this key is lost or changed, all stored credentials become unreadable
#          and users will need to re-enter passwords / re-authenticate OAuth accounts.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=ENC[AES256_GCM,data:ukVN6OeR0H0eI984s1YaOhdL0J+pNuEechJAsNTV225fgyezUj+mGIfT5vYKyyf/KtckMwAMeAT6u4xrqLeVzw==,iv:5diPZJVv6CGy+Ce/VAARh/ibew8NyI6mrPDbZZZYSaI=,tag:A07Mcb/8ezdL9IcCeLdpJQ==,type:str]
# ── Database & Redis (advanced — external servers) ────────────────────────────
# By default MailFlow runs its own bundled Postgres and Redis, and these are
# already wired up. Only set these to use EXISTING servers elsewhere on your
# network (e.g. on a non-standard port). If you do, stop/remove the bundled
# `postgres` and `redis` services so they don't run unused.
# DB_HOST=postgres
# DB_PORT=5432
# DB_NAME=mailflow
# DB_USER=mailflow
# REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379
# ── Storage & permissions (bind mounts / Unraid) ──────────────────────────────
# By default the Postgres and Redis data live in Docker-managed named volumes.
# To store them on a host path instead (e.g. an Unraid appdata share), point these
# at that path, create/chown it to PUID:PGID first, then set PUID/PGID so the
# containers write files as that owner. On Unraid, PUID=99 and PGID=100.
# Leaving PUID/PGID unset keeps the default (root) behaviour with named volumes.
# POSTGRES_DATA=/mnt/user/appdata/mailflow/postgres
# REDIS_DATA=/mnt/user/appdata/mailflow/redis
# PUID=99
# PGID=100
# ── Microsoft personal accounts (Outlook.com / Hotmail) ──────────────────────
# Personal Microsoft accounts (Outlook.com, Hotmail, Live, MSN) connect with no
# setup: when no Microsoft app that accepts them is configured under Settings →
# Email providers, the device-code sign-in uses MailFlow's own registered public
# app. Work and school accounts still need your own app. You sign in on
# Microsoft's own page; Microsoft sends the tokens straight to your server, and
# MailFlow's maintainers never see mail or tokens. Set false to stop offering the
# shared app, for example if your policy requires your own Entra app
# registration (see the README). Accounts already connected through it keep
# working. The switch in Settings → Email providers, once used,
# takes precedence over this value.
MS_BUILTIN_APP=
# ── Your own Microsoft Entra app (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# Needed for work and school accounts, and for personal accounts through your
# own app instead of the shared one. The usual place to set it up is Settings →
# Email providers (see the README); these lines are the alternative. They work
# on Docker as well as native installs. Values saved under Settings → Email
# providers take precedence over these, field by field. An empty value counts as
# not set; MS_TENANT_ID then defaults to common. If your docker-compose.yml has
# no MS_CLIENT_ID line, download it again, or these never reach the backend.
# MS_CLIENT_ID=
# MS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MS_TENANT_ID=common
# MS_REDIRECT_URI=https://your-domain/oauth/microsoft/callback
# ── Google OAuth for Gmail / Workspace accounts (optional) ───────────────────
# Lets you connect Gmail and Google Workspace mailboxes with OAuth instead of an
# app password. Workspace admins can disable app passwords entirely, in which
# case OAuth is the only way to reach those mailboxes.
#
# Setup, in Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials:
#   1. Create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID of type "Web application"
#   2. Add this authorized redirect URI (must match GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI exactly):
#        https://your-domain/oauth/google/callback
#   3. On the OAuth consent screen, add the scope https://mail.google.com/
#
# https://mail.google.com/ is a RESTRICTED scope, so consent screen setup matters:
#
#   Internal (project owned by a Google Workspace org)
#     No verification needed, refresh tokens do not expire. Only accounts in that
#     org can connect. This is the option that works for self-hosting.
#
#   External + Testing
#     Works immediately, but Google revokes refresh tokens after 7 DAYS, so every
#     connected account must be reconnected weekly. Fine for trying it out only.
#
#   External + In production
#     Long-lived tokens, but restricted scopes require full Google verification
#     including a security audit.
#
# Consumer @gmail.com accounts cannot be covered by an Internal app. Use an app
# password for those; this flow is aimed at Workspace mailboxes, where admins can
# disable app passwords entirely.
#
# Then enter the client ID, secret and redirect URI under Settings → Email
# providers, or set them here. These lines work on Docker as well as native
# installs. Values saved under Settings → Email providers take precedence over
# these, field by field, and an empty value counts as not set. If your
# docker-compose.yml has no GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID line, download it again, or these
# never reach the backend.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://your-domain/oauth/google/callback
# ── Web Push Notifications (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Enables native OS / PWA push notifications for new mail.
# Generate a key pair once with:  npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# VAPID_SUBJECT must be either a mailto: address or your APP_URL.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@yourdomain.com
# ── Let's Encrypt (optional — only needed with --profile https) ───────────────
# Only used when running: docker compose --profile https up -d --build
# Requires a real public domain with DNS pointing at this server.
DOMAIN=mail.yourdomain.com
ACME_EMAIL=you@yourdomain.com
# ── Source-build tuning (advanced) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Base image for the frontend (nginx) stage when building from source. Some
# hosts with older kernels (e.g. NAS units) cannot execute binaries from the
# newest Alpine package repo — `apk add` in the build dies with "killed by
# signal 9". Point this at an older tag to fix that. Default: nginx:alpine.
# NGINX_BASE=nginx:1.27-alpine
# ── IMAP tuning ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Max always-on IMAP connections MailFlow keeps per provider host. Each enabled
# account normally holds one persistent (IDLE) connection, so many accounts on
# ONE mail server can exceed the server's per-user/per-IP connection limit (e.g.
# Dovecot's mail_max_userip_connections, default 10), causing sync errors and a
# snippet-indexer backoff loop. Set this to your provider's limit and MailFlow
# keeps that many accounts on live push and polls the rest on the sync interval.
# Empty or 0 = unlimited (default; unchanged behavior). Only set it if you run
# many accounts on one connection-limited server.
IMAP_MAX_PERSISTENT_PER_HOST=
# Disk space for images loaded through MailFlow's image proxy, fitted to the
# email and kept per user so another device or a later view does not fetch
# them from the sender again. In MB; the least recently used go first.
IMAGE_CACHE_MAX_MB=512
# ── Experimental ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Div-based email renderer (replaces iframe). Set to true on a dev instance to
# test before the iframe path is removed. Do not enable in production until the
# full email test matrix has been validated.
VITE_EMAIL_DIV_RENDER=false
